<section xml:id="sec_semicircular-wire" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Application: Semicircular Wire</title>
	
<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section were designed to be implemented in a 2-hour interactive studio classroom, after students have completed:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_charge-density"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_charge-diagrams"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_e-field_many"/>
			</p>
		</li>
		<li>
			<p>
				<xref ref="sec_elec-superposition"/>
			</p>
		</li>
	</ul>
		<p>
		<xref ref="sec_straight-wire"/> makes an excellent follow-up!
	</p>
  </assemblage>

  <subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>
	
  </subsubsection>

<activity xml:id="class_elec-approx">
		<title>Approximating a Wire</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			A charged wire with length <m>L</m> is oriented horizontally. The wire has a positive uniform charge density (the charge is spread out uniformly along the one-dimensional wire).
		</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Make a charge diagram of the wire that shows the location of the charges on the wire.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Divide your wire into six equal, point-like pieces.  Use superposition to sketch the net electric field at each of the following points:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<p>
							A horizontal distance <m>L</m> from one end of the wire.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							A vertical distance <m>L</m> from the center of the wire.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							A vertical distance <m>L</m> from one end of the wire.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use <url href="https://beav.es/charges-fields">this simulation</url> to check if your sketch is correct by creating a similar distribution using point-like charges
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</activity>

	<activity xml:id="class_elec-approx2">
		<title>Approximating a Semicircle</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			A uniformly charged semi-circular wire has radius <m>R</m> has a total charge <m>+Q</m>. Determine the electric field at the center of the semi-circle using the following method
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				<p>
					Divide the wire into at least 6 point-like charges.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Find a symbolic expression for the electric field at the center of the semi-circle due to each of these point-like charges.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Add together the electric field due to each of these six charges.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
Make sense of both the magnitude and the direction of your electric field.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					How much charge is on a segment of the wire that covers a very small angle <m>d\theta</m>?
				</p>
			</li>
		</ol>
		</statement>
		
	</activity>

	<activity xml:id="class_semicircle-elec">
		<title>The Semicircle</title>
		<introduction>
			<p>
			
		</p>
		<p>
			A uniformly charged semi-circular wire has radius <m>R</m> has a total charge <m>+Q</m>.
			</p>
			<figure xml:id="fig_elec-semicircle">
		<title>Semicircular Wire Figure</title>
    	<caption>A charged semicircular wire.</caption>
    		<image source="elec-semicircle.png" width = "80%" >
          <description>A semicircle starting at the top and moving counterclockwise to the bottom.  dq is labeled near the bottom at an angle theta from the horizontal.</description>
        </image>
    </figure>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<alert>Chop:</alert> In the previous activity, you chopped the semicircle first into six small pieces and then into infinitesimally small pieces.  If you do not have one already, write an equation that relates <m>dq</m> and <m>d\theta</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<alert>Multiply:</alert> What is the electric field <m>\vec{dE}</m> at the center of the circle due to the segment of the wire that covers a very small angle <m>d\theta</m>?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					<alert>Add:</alert> What is the net electric field at the center of the circle?  What direction does it point?  Does your answer make sense?
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Compare your result to the answer you got when you only chopped the wire up into six discrete pieces.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</task>
	</activity>


	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

